"Explains a customer's movement toward or away from change through the pushes, pulls, habits and anxieties present in a situation.\n\nUse when: Understand why a customer adopted, delayed or rejected an alternative. Explore barriers that benefit-focused messaging alone does not address.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs a general list of product advantages without evidence from a switching situation.\n\nVisual grammar: Four named force regions distinguish push, pull, habit and anxiety. A change boundary or directional arrangement separates enabling and restraining forces. Notes retain concrete circumstances from a customer account.\n\nBuild: Select one actual switching episode and reconstruct its context. Identify pressures away from the old situation and attraction to the new. Separate habitual inertia from specific anxieties and check the evidence.\n\nCheck: Forces describe the customer's circumstances rather than seller assumptions. Habit and anxiety retain different meanings. The account includes what happened before and after the decision.\n\nPitfalls: Treating all reluctance as ignorance of the product benefits. Inventing a numerical force balance from qualitative interview notes.\n\nExample: A commuter considering cycling describes congestion as push, independence as pull, driving habit as inertia and road safety as anxiety.\n\nAlternatives: Job map, Force-field analysis."
